        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Fitness Trackers</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these trackers. They were chosen from maker product pages, manuals
            and Amazon listings, subscription terms, recall records and independent hands-on reviews
            — chiefly Tom's Guide, TechRadar and Live Science — checked on 19 October 2026, and the
            claims on this page are limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from the shapes people actually choose between: a screenless band, a slim
            band with a screen, a fitness-first GPS watch, a full smartwatch, a subscription
            wearable and a ring. For each we looked for a current model that is sold on Amazon US
            under the brand's own listing, so the link on the card goes somewhere real. Then we
            checked each printed figure in at least two places. Battery claims were the most common
            conflict: reviewers' real-world figures often fall short of the makers', so the cards
            quote the maker's figure, labeled as such, only where an independent source repeated
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Several good trackers fell out on that Amazon-first rule. The
            <strong>Garmin Cirqa</strong>, a $199.99 screenless band launched in July 2026 with a
            claimed 10-day battery and no required subscription, is well reviewed, but on 19 October
            we found only third-party straps for it on Amazon US, not the band itself; it is sold by
            Garmin and other retailers. Google's new <strong>Fitbit Edge</strong>, announced on 12
            October at $179.99 with built-in GPS and a screen, was on pre-order with deliveries
            starting 27 October, so it was not yet on sale; it replaces the
            <strong>Fitbit Charge 6</strong>, whose Amazon listing showed no price when we checked.
            We will revisit the Edge once it ships. The <strong>Polar Loop</strong> and the
            <strong>Amazfit Helio Strap</strong> are both on Amazon as subscription-free screenless
            bands, but at the same or higher prices than the Fitbit Air they overlap almost
            completely, so they are mentioned rather than carded. We did not check Samsung's
            watches or other smart rings in enough depth to card them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every brand on this page was searched in the CPSC recall database by recall title,
            manufacturer name and recall description. We found no recall for any product listed.
            Fitbit has two past recalls, neither for a current product: the Force wristband in 2014
            over skin irritation, and the Ionic smartwatch in 2022 over a burn hazard. Apple's
            recalls on record concern laptops, batteries, plug adapters and a speaker, not watches.
            We found none for Garmin, Amazfit, WHOOP or Oura.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscriptions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Which Fitness Trackers Need a Subscription?</h2>
<p>
            This is the question that most changes the real price of a fitness tracker. There are
            three models: no subscription at all, an optional subscription that adds coaching and
            extra analysis, and a membership without which the device does very little.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Tracker</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Subscription</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Published price on 19 Oct 2026</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fitbit Air, Fitbit Inspire 3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Google Health Premium (formerly Fitbit Premium), optional</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$9.99 a month or $99 a year, as reported by Tom's Guide; a three-month trial is included</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Garmin vívoactive 6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Garmin Connect+, optional</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$6.99 a month or $69.99 a year, as reported by Tom's Guide</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Apple Watch SE 3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Apple Fitness+, optional</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not checked; the watch's own tracking is free</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazfit Bip 6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Zepp Aura, optional</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not checked; reviewers say wellness reports need it</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">WHOOP 5.0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Membership, required; the device comes with it</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">One $199, Peak $239, Life $359 a year</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Oura Ring 5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Oura Membership, needed for most features</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$5.99 a month or $69.99 a year in the US, per Oura; first month free for new members</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Do the two-year arithmetic.</strong> Most people keep a tracker for at least two
            years, so compare the purchase price plus two years of whatever you would actually pay.
            On the prices above, a Fitbit Air with no subscription is about $100 over two years; with
            Premium it is about $300. An Oura Ring 5 in silver with an annual membership is about
            $540 ($399 plus two years at $69.99). WHOOP One is $398 over two years at the annual
            price, and Peak $478. Those are simple sums from the published prices, not quotes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What an optional subscription usually adds.</strong> On the Fitbit, Garmin and
            Amazfit devices, steps, heart rate, sleep and workouts are recorded and shown for free.
            The paid tiers add AI coaching, training plans, longer-term trend analysis and some
            extra scores. Reviewers generally describe them as useful for some people and easy to
            skip for others, so take the trial, then decide.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What happens if you stop paying.</strong> On a subscription-required device
            such as WHOOP, the hardware is effectively part of the membership. On Oura, Oura says
            canceling keeps your saved data, but the experience without a membership is limited.
            Read the maker's current terms before buying either.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="accuracy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How Accurate Are Fitness Trackers?</h2>
<p>
            Accurate enough to show trends, not accurate enough to treat any single number as a
            measurement. How far you can trust a figure depends on what it is.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Steps</h3>
          <p>
            Wrist and ring trackers count steps largely from the swing and jolt of your arm. On a
            free walk they do well. They under-count when your arms stop moving: holding treadmill
            handrails, pushing a stroller, or typing on a walking pad. Our explainer on
            <a href="/does-a-fitness-tracker-count-steps-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether a fitness tracker counts steps on a treadmill</a>
            goes through each case, and our guide to
            <a href="/best-fitness-trackers-for-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">fitness trackers for a walking pad</a>
            covers the waist-worn and clip-on fixes. A ring is on your hand, so it has the same
            problem as a wrist.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Heart rate</h3>
          <p>
            Every tracker on this page reads heart rate optically, by shining light into the skin
            and watching blood flow. That works best at rest and during steady effort. It is less
            reliable in fast intervals, when the band is loose, in the cold, and during movements
            that flex the wrist, such as lifting. A chest strap reads the heart's electrical signal
            instead and is the better tool for <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>interval training</a>. Our comparison of
            <a href="/chest-strap-vs-wrist-heart-rate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">chest strap and wrist heart rate</a>
            explains where the wrist lags and how to wear it for the cleanest reading, and most of
            these trackers can pair with a strap if you need one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Distance and pace</h3>
          <p>
            Outdoors, a tracker with built-in GPS (the vívoactive 6, <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apple-watch/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Apple Watch</a> SE 3 and Bip 6
            here) measures distance itself. The Fitbit bands have no GPS of their
            own and borrow your phone's, as most screenless bands and rings do, so leave the phone at
            home and outdoor distance becomes an estimate. Indoors, every
            tracker estimates distance from stride, so a treadmill console that has been checked is
            usually the better figure; our
            <a href="/treadmill-calibration/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calibration guide</a>
            shows how to check one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Calories</h3>
          <p>
            Calorie burn is the weakest figure on any tracker. It is calculated from heart rate,
            movement and the body details you enter, using a model, and different devices disagree
            for the same session. Use it to compare your own days with each other, not as a number
            to eat back. Our page on
            <a href="/are-treadmill-calories-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether treadmill calorie counts are accurate</a>
            explains why consoles and wearables rarely agree.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sleep and readiness scores</h3>
          <p>
            Sleep stages, recovery, readiness and strain scores are each maker's own estimates,
            built from heart rate, heart rate variability, movement and skin temperature. They are
            not comparable between brands, and a score of 70 on one app does not mean what 70 means
            on another. They are most useful as a personal baseline: a run of low scores after poor
            sleep or hard training is worth noticing; a single low score is not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="band-watch-or-ring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Band, Watch or Ring: Which Shape Suits You?</h2>
<p>
            The shape decides more about daily life with a tracker than any sensor does.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Screenless band (Fitbit Air, WHOOP)</h3>
          <p>
            No display, no notifications, nothing to look at; everything happens in the phone app.
            Screenless bands are light, comfortable to sleep in and last a week or more. The cost is
            that you cannot glance at your heart rate or a timer mid-workout, and outdoor distance
            depends on carrying your phone. <strong>Best for</strong> people who want data without
            distraction, and for sleep tracking.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Band with a screen (Fitbit Inspire 3)</h3>
          <p>
            A slim strap with a small display for time, steps, heart rate and notifications. Cheap
            and long-lasting, but the screen is small and there is no built-in GPS.
            <strong>Best for</strong> a first tracker, and for anyone who wants to see their step
            count during the day.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fitness-first watch (Garmin vívoactive 6, Amazfit Bip 6)</h3>
          <p>
            A bigger screen, built-in GPS and workout modes you start from the wrist, with battery
            measured in days or weeks rather than hours. Fewer smartwatch apps than an Apple Watch.
            <strong>Best for</strong> people who run, cycle or walk outdoors and want pace and
            distance without a phone.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Smartwatch (Apple Watch SE 3)</h3>
          <p>
            A phone on your wrist that also tracks fitness. The richest apps and notifications, and
            the shortest battery, which means charging every day and either missing some sleep data
            or charging at another time. <strong>Best for</strong> iPhone owners who want one device
            for everything.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ring (Oura Ring 5)</h3>
          <p>
            The least intrusive of all to wear, especially at night. Rings are strong for sleep and
            resting heart rate, but they have no screen, are poorly suited to wearing while lifting
            weights, need a careful sizing, and count steps from your hand.
            <strong>Best for</strong> people who will not wear anything on the wrist, and for sleep.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Battery Life: What the Claims Mean</h2>
<p>
            Every battery figure on this page is the maker's claim under its own test conditions,
            and the makers say so in their footnotes. Real life is usually shorter, because GPS,
            an always-on display, frequent notifications, blood-oxygen readings and cold weather all
            drain the battery faster.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Tracker</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Maker's claim</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What changes it most</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">WHOOP 5.0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">14+ days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Charges while worn, so the gap matters less</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazfit Bip 6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Up to 14 days typical, 6 days heavy use</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">GPS workouts and screen brightness</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Garmin vívoactive 6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Up to 11 days; up to 21 hours of GPS</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Always-on display, GPS mode, music</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fitbit Inspire 3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Up to 10 days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Notifications and screen use</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Oura Ring 5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 to 9 days, depending on size</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Ring size (smaller rings, smaller battery) and features</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fitbit Air</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Up to 7 days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Workout tracking and background features</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Apple Watch SE 3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Up to 18 hours; 32 in Low Power Mode</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Workouts with music, cellular use</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Battery matters for more than convenience. Sleep tracking only works if the tracker is
            on your wrist or finger overnight, so a device you charge at night will have gaps. A
            week-long battery also makes it far more likely you will wear the tracker every day,
            and consistent wear is what makes trends meaningful. If you are choosing between two
            otherwise similar trackers, the one you will not have to think about charging usually
            wins.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="treadmill-and-home-gym" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Using a Fitness Tracker on a Treadmill and in a Home Gym</h2>
<p>
            Indoor training is where trackers are most often misread. A few habits make the numbers
            far more useful.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Start an indoor workout, not an outdoor one.</strong> Indoor walk and run
              modes estimate distance from motion instead of searching for GPS, which on a treadmill
              produces nonsense.</li>
            <li><strong>Let your arms swing.</strong> <a href="/should-you-hold-the-handrails-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Holding the rails</a> or the console stops a wrist
              tracker seeing your steps and lowers your effort. If you need the rails for balance,
              trust the console's distance and expect a low step count.</li>
            <li><strong>Treat the console as the record for distance and pace.</strong> Then use
              the tracker for heart rate and time in zones. Our guide to
              <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill heart rate zones</a>
              explains how to set them, and
              <a href="/zone-2-training-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">zone 2 training on a treadmill</a>
              shows a common way to use them.</li>
            <li><strong>Pair a strap for intervals and lifting.</strong> Wrist readings struggle with
              fast changes and with gripping a bar. A chest strap gives a cleaner trace, and many
              trackers will display it.</li>
            <li><strong>Connect where you can.</strong> Some treadmills share speed and distance with
              a watch directly. If you use an Apple Watch, our guide to
              <a href="/how-to-connect-apple-watch-to-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">connecting an Apple Watch to a treadmill</a>
              covers GymKit and app-based options.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For strength training, accept that most trackers record time and heart rate well and
            reps and sets poorly. Log the lifts yourself. If you are building a home gym around a
            treadmill, our
            <a href="/best-cardio-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best cardio machines for home</a>
            guide compares the main options, and if body composition is the goal rather than
            activity, our guide to the
            <a href="/best-smart-scales/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best smart scales</a>
            explains what a bathroom scale can add, and how little a single body-fat reading means.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you mainly run and train to pace,</strong> a dedicated running watch with
            better GPS and training tools is the better buy; our
            <a href="/best-running-watches-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running watch roundup</a>
            covers the treadmill side.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you train by heart rate in intervals,</strong> buy a chest strap first. It is
            cheaper than most trackers and more accurate for that job; our
            <a href="/best-heart-rate-monitors-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">heart rate monitor picks</a>
            list the current straps and arm bands.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you only want a step count at a walking desk,</strong> a $25 clip-on
            pedometer will do it better than anything on this page, for the reasons in our
            <a href="/best-fitness-trackers-for-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad tracker guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you already own a recent phone and want to see whether tracking helps,</strong>
            try its built-in step counting and a free app for a month before buying anything. A
            phone in your pocket counts steps well, and you will learn what you actually look at.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you need medical-grade readings,</strong> none of these is the answer. A
            consumer tracker is a fitness and wellness tool, and decisions about a heart rhythm,
            blood oxygen or sleep disorder belong with a clinician and the equipment they use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best fitness tracker in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Google Fitbit Air. It costs about $100, lasts about a week per charge, is light enough to wear overnight and records steps, heart rate, sleep and workouts without a subscription. If you want a screen and built-in GPS, the Garmin vivoactive 6 is the step up, and iPhone owners who want a full smartwatch should look at the Apple Watch SE 3.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which fitness trackers do not need a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Of the trackers on this page, the Fitbit Air, Fitbit Inspire 3, Garmin vivoactive 6, Apple Watch SE 3 and Amazfit Bip 6 all record your core data for free, with optional paid tiers for coaching and extra analysis. WHOOP requires a paid membership, and the Oura Ring needs its membership for most features. Check each maker's current terms before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Whoop worth it compared with a Fitbit or Garmin?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you want WHOOP's strain and recovery coaching and accept paying every year. On WHOOP's published prices the cheapest plan is $199 a year, so two years costs about $398, against about $100 for a Fitbit Air with no subscription. A Fitbit or Garmin covers steps, heart rate, sleep and workouts for far less.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How accurate are fitness trackers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Accurate enough to show trends, not to treat one number as a measurement. Step counts are good on a free walk but drop when your arms are still, as on treadmill rails or at a walking desk. Wrist heart rate is best at rest and steady effort and weaker in fast intervals. Calories, sleep stages and readiness scores are estimates from each maker's model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do fitness trackers count steps on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if your arms swing normally. Wrist and ring trackers count steps largely from arm movement, so holding the handrails or typing on a walking pad lowers the count. For distance, a treadmill console that has been checked is usually more reliable than a tracker's indoor estimate. A phone in a trouser pocket or a waist clip counts better when your hands are busy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which fitness tracker has the longest battery life?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Of the picks here, WHOOP 5.0 claims 14 days or more and the Amazfit Bip 6 up to 14 days in typical use, followed by the Garmin vivoactive 6 at up to 11 days and the Fitbit Inspire 3 at up to 10. These are the makers' figures; GPS, an always-on screen and notifications shorten them. The Apple Watch SE 3 is rated at up to 18 hours.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Fitness Tracker</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these is easy to avoid before you order and costs money or data afterwards.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing sticker prices and ignoring the subscription</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A tracker that needs a membership costs its yearly fee every year you use it. Add up the purchase price and two years of whatever you would actually pay before comparing. On published prices, a subscription-free band can cost a quarter of what a membership device costs over the same period.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating calories and scores as measurements</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Calorie burn, sleep stages and readiness scores are estimates from each maker's model, and different devices disagree for the same day. Use them to compare your own days and weeks, not to decide how much to eat or whether to train. Steps and resting heart rate trends are the more dependable figures.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a wrist tracker to count desk or rail-holding steps</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Wrist and ring trackers count from arm movement, so they under-count when your hands are on a keyboard or handrail. If that is where most of your walking happens, clip a tracker or pedometer to your waistband, carry your phone in a pocket, or use the treadmill's own distance.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a tracker you will not wear</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A tracker only builds useful trends if it is on you every day and night. Short battery life, a bulky case or a strap you dislike all lead to gaps. Pick for comfort and charging habits first, features second, and wear it snugly just above the wrist bone for the best heart-rate reading.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

