        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">For most people, the Fitbit Air is the fitness tracker to buy</strong>:
            about $100, a week of battery, comfortable overnight and useful without a subscription. If
            you want a screen and GPS on your wrist, the Garmin vívoactive 6 does that with no monthly
            fee, and the Amazfit Bip 6 does most of it for well under $100. iPhone owners who want
            one device for everything should choose the Apple Watch SE 3 and accept daily charging.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            WHOOP and Oura are good at what they do, but you pay every year, so buy them for the
            recovery and sleep analysis, not for steps. Whatever you choose, read trends rather than
            single numbers, and use a chest strap when heart rate really matters —
            <a href="/chest-strap-vs-wrist-heart-rate/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">our chest strap vs wrist guide</a>
            explains when.
          </p>
        </div>

